# Independently authored PG `to_char()` partial-
# implementation CORRUPTION-class sentinel slice for the
# GPL MTR-format runner. NOT copied or adapted from
# MySQL's mysql-test/.
#
# Existing coverage (read carefully — DO NOT duplicate):
#   - `TO_CHAR(DATE '2023-04-05', 'YYYY-MM-DD')` at
#     function_basic.test:118 — ONE shape, pinning ONLY
#     the basic-WORKS spec `YYYY-MM-DD`. No coverage of
#     any other format code, no number form, no overflow
#     handling, no "kept literally" partial-impl
#     mechanism. Verified via grep across `tests/main/
#     *.test` for `to_char\(` / `TO_CHAR\(` — that one
#     line is the entire prior surface.
#
# Genuinely ZERO prior coverage on these shapes (grep
# verified across tests/main/*.test):
#   - `to_char(num, fmt)` numeric form — ANY format spec,
#     including the WORKS subset (digit/zero slots, S
#     sign, L currency, '.' decimal, ',' group sep).
#   - `to_char(num, fmt)` overflow behavior (`###`
#     width-fail indicator).
#   - `to_char(num, fmt)` KEPT-LITERAL partial-impl bug
#     for B (blank-if-zero), RN (Roman numeral), FM (no-
#     fill), EEEE (scientific), TH (ordinal), XXX (hex),
#     PR (paren-on-negative — actually IMPLEMENTED!),
#     MI (left-sign — actually IMPLEMENTED!).
#   - `to_char(date/timestamp, fmt)` form for ANY spec
#     other than the one `YYYY-MM-DD` pin already in
#     function_basic.test.
#   - `to_char(timestamp, fmt)` KEPT-LITERAL partial-
#     impl bug for `Day`, `Month`, `Mon`, `Dy`, `AM`,
#     `PM`, `Q`, `WW`, `DOY`, `D`, `CC`, `TZ`, `MS`,
#     `IYYY`, `IW`, `ID`, and the quoted-literal `"T"`
#     escape.
#
# Pre-existing pattern fingerprints this slice joins:
#   - CORRUPTION-class family: PR #421 (DEFAULT subquery
#     stored as text literal), PR #432 (TIMESTAMP(n)
#     precision silent-drop), PR #438 (EXPLAIN option
#     modifiers FORMAT/ANALYZE/BUFFERS silent-drop),
#     PR #440 (PG printf-style `format()` silently
#     re-routed to MySQL numeric `FORMAT(num, dec)`).
#     Shared mechanism: the wire reports success, the
#     value/output is structurally a STRING but its
#     content is WRONG. No error, no warning. Apps that
#     pipe the output into downstream parsers, report
#     templates, or label generators get garbage that
#     LOOKS LIKE a format example back — particularly
#     hard to notice in eyeballed test runs because the
#     output "kind of" looks right.
#
# THE BUG (CORRUPTION class):
#
#   PG's `to_char()` has two function arms:
#     `to_char(numeric, text)` — numeric formatting with
#         template patterns: 9 (digit), 0 (zero-pad),
#         . (decimal), , (group), S (sign), L (currency
#         locale), B (blank-if-zero), RN (Roman),
#         EEEE (scientific), FM (no-fill / strip
#         padding), TH (ordinal), XXX (hex), PR (paren
#         on negative), MI (left-justified sign).
#     `to_char(timestamp, text)` — date/time formatting
#         with template patterns: YYYY/YY (year), MM
#         (month num), DD (day-of-month), Day (day-name
#         padded), Month (month-name padded), Mon/Dy
#         (abbreviated), HH24/HH12 (hour), MI (minute),
#         SS (second), MS (millisecond), AM/PM (period
#         marker), Q (quarter), WW (week-of-year),
#         DOY (day-of-year), D (day-of-week 1-7),
#         CC (century), IYYY/IW/ID (ISO week-year
#         family), TZ (timezone abbreviation), plus
#         `"..."` for quoted literal passthrough.
#
#   In zeta, ONLY a partial subset of these template
#   patterns is implemented. The OTHERS are passed
#   through to the output AS LITERAL TEXT — the parser
#   sees an unknown spec, fails to match it to a
#   handler, and the spec characters fall through to
#   the result string unchanged. No error. No warning.
#
#   This is materially worse than the more-common
#   "spec silently ignored" shape (where the spec would
#   simply not appear in the output): here the spec
#   characters APPEAR in the output verbatim, so the
#   user gets back a string that LOOKS LIKE a format
#   example. `to_char(now(), 'Day Month YYYY')` returns
#   the string `'Day Month 2024'` — a partly-rendered
#   template that is trivial to mistake for "almost
#   right" in a glance-pass through a report.
#
#   Overflow has a separate (but also non-erroring)
#   shape: `to_char(1234.56, '999.99')` returns
#   `'###.##'` (the integer part doesn't fit the 3-
#   digit slot — three `#` chars replace the value).
#   Real PG returns `'1234.56'` for the same call
#   (PG uses the `.99` slot count as a HINT, not a
#   hard cap on the integer side, and emits the full
#   value if it fits the larger slot count).
#
# Why this is CORRUPTION-class (joins PR #421/#432/
# #438/#440):
#   - Wrong content, NO error signal. Wire returns a
#     plain VARCHAR result that LOOKS like a normal
#     format output — only the spec characters are
#     visible-but-wrong.
#   - Report generators / CDC label formatters / audit
#     log timestamp renderers emit garbage that propagates
#     to dashboards, exported CSVs, and operator emails
#     before anyone notices the spec chars.
#   - Django ORM raw queries, dashboard SQL templates,
#     and tooling that emit PG-style `to_char()` (because
#     PG-on-the-wire is the contract) will silently
#     start emitting `'Day Month 2024'` instead of
#     `'Friday March 2024'`. The downstream consumer
#     gets a string that has the right SHAPE but wrong
#     CONTENT.
#   - Width-overflow `###` shape is the SAME class but
#     visually different — it looks more like an obvious
#     "this is wrong" but PG never emits it (PG widens
#     the field), so any app that ported `to_char(num,
#     '999.99')` from PG will get `'###.##'` instead of
#     the expected value.
#
# Wire divergence check: the `to_char` function dispatch
# is in zeta-executor (shared scalar registry). Both
# pgwire and mysqlwire reach the same impl — the
# partial-impl parser is the same code path. Probed via
# mysqlwire (this slice's wire); expected identical
# behavior on pgwire.
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: CORRUPTION-class BUG (numeric form) —
#     `to_char(num, fmt)` keeps UNIMPLEMENTED format
#     codes in the output verbatim instead of erroring
#     or stripping them. Implemented codes: 0/9 (digit
#     slots), '.', ',', S (sign), L (currency → '$'
#     USD only), PR (paren-on-negative), MI (left sign).
#     Silently-kept-literal codes (incomplete list):
#     B (blank-if-zero), RN (Roman numeral), FM (no-
#     fill modifier), EEEE (scientific), TH (ordinal),
#     XXX (hex). Workaround: format numbers in
#     application code; do NOT pass any PG template
#     pattern other than the implemented subset above.
#   # zeta-adapt: CORRUPTION-class BUG (numeric form,
#     overflow) — `to_char(num, fmt)` emits `###` chars
#     when the integer part exceeds the slot count
#     (e.g. `to_char(1234.5, '999.9')` → `'###.#'`).
#     Real PG emits the full value (`'1234.5'`).
#     Workaround: size the format string for the
#     maximum expected value, or use the digit-AND-
#     zero pattern (`'0000.0'`) which avoids the
#     overflow indicator.
#   # zeta-adapt: CORRUPTION-class BUG (date form) —
#     `to_char(date/timestamp, fmt)` keeps UNIMPLEMENTED
#     format codes in the output verbatim. Implemented
#     codes: YYYY, YY, MM, DD, HH24, HH12 (partial —
#     digits run together with next chars), MI (minute),
#     SS. Silently-kept-literal codes (incomplete list):
#     Day, Month, Mon, Dy, AM, PM, Q, WW, DOY, D, CC,
#     TZ, MS, IYYY, IW, ID. The quoted-literal `"..."`
#     escape is ALSO not implemented — the double-quote
#     characters appear in the output instead of being
#     stripped. Workaround: build date/time strings in
#     application code or via the MySQL `DATE_FORMAT`
#     function (which uses `%`-prefixed specs and is
#     fully implemented).
#   # zeta-adapt: `to_char(NULL, fmt)` returns SQL NULL
#     (pinned). This is the one well-behaved case in
#     the function and matches PG semantics.
#
# This slice pins (TWELVE shapes — eight CORRUPTION
# shapes split across numeric / date / overflow buckets,
# two WORKS-baseline pins, two PARTIAL-WORKS-but-
# noteworthy pins, and one NULL-passthrough support
# pin):
#
#   WORKS-baseline pins (lock the implemented subset
#   so any future regression in the WORKING codes is
#   visible alongside the partial-impl drift):
#
#     Shape W1 — `to_char(1234.5678, '0000.0000')` →
#       '1234.5678'. The digit-and-zero pattern works
#       (no overflow because the slot count matches).
#     Shape W2 — `to_char(DATE '2024-03-15', 'YYYY-MM-
#       DD')` → '2024-03-15'. Basic date format works
#       (the only PG date spec pinned anywhere prior).
#
#   CORRUPTION-class BUG — numeric form, KEPT-LITERAL
#   (headline):
#
#     Shape A — `to_char(1, 'RN')` → 'RN' (real PG:
#       '          I'). The Roman-numeral format code
#       is kept LITERALLY in the output. Particularly
#       insidious because the result LOOKS LIKE the
#       user passed the wrong arg.
#     Shape B — `to_char(1234.5678, 'B99999')` →
#       'B 1234' (real PG: ' 1234' — the B modifier
#       means "render blanks if value is zero", non-
#       printing for nonzero values). The B character
#       leaks into the output.
#     Shape C — `to_char(1234.5678, 'FM999G999D99')`
#       → 'FM  1,234.56' (real PG: '1,234.56' — the
#       FM modifier means "no padding"). The FM
#       characters leak into the output AND padding
#       is not stripped (the implemented codes G/D
#       happen to work as group-sep/decimal-point).
#     Shape D — `to_char(1234, '9999TH')` → '1234TH'
#       (real PG: '1234TH'). LOOKS correct by
#       coincidence — `1234` ends in `4` so the
#       English ordinal happens to be `TH` — but the
#       TH chars are kept literally regardless of the
#       trailing digit. Compare with Shape D2 which
#       proves the literal-kept mechanism.
#     Shape D2 — `to_char(1, '9TH')` → '1TH' (real
#       PG: '1ST'). PROOF that TH is literal-kept,
#       not implemented: the ordinal for 1 is `ST`,
#       not `TH`. zeta returns `TH` regardless.
#     Shape E — `to_char(123, 'XXX')` → 'XXX' (real
#       PG: ' 7b' — hex 7B for decimal 123). The X
#       hex-digit specs are kept literally; no hex
#       conversion happens.
#
#   CORRUPTION-class BUG — numeric form, OVERFLOW:
#
#     Shape F — `to_char(1234.5678, '999.99')` →
#       '###.##' (real PG: '1234.57' — PG widens the
#       field rather than emitting overflow chars).
#       The `#` overflow indicator is the most
#       visually-obvious failure mode but PG never
#       emits it.
#     Shape F2 — `to_char(1.5e10, '9.99EEEE')` →
#       '#.##' (real PG: '  1.50e+10' — scientific
#       notation). Combined fault: EEEE is not
#       implemented (would be kept literal alone)
#       AND the value overflows the 1-digit integer
#       slot (so `#` appears even though the EEEE
#       chars are stripped, which is a third
#       inconsistency on top).
#
#   CORRUPTION-class BUG — date form, KEPT-LITERAL:
#
#     Shape G — `to_char(DATE '2024-03-15', 'Day, DD
#       Month YYYY')` → 'Day, 15 Month 2024' (real PG:
#       'Friday   , 15 March      2024'). HEADLINE
#       date-form CORRUPTION — the `Day` and `Month`
#       full-name specs are kept LITERALLY in the
#       output. Report templates and PDF/CSV
#       generators will emit the literal strings
#       `Day` and `Month` instead of weekday/month
#       names.
#     Shape H — `to_char(TIMESTAMP '2024-03-15
#       02:25:30', 'HH12:MI:SS AM')` → '0212:25:30
#       AM' (real PG: '02:25:30 AM'). Two separate
#       faults visible: (1) HH12 emits the `02` but
#       the `:` separator is consumed by the parser
#       attempting `MI`-after-`H` which then matches
#       again — the digits run together; (2) the
#       `AM` period-marker spec is kept LITERALLY
#       in the output. Note: real PG would also emit
#       `AM` for a 02:25:30 time, but zeta emits `AM`
#       for the 14:25:30 PM time as well (see Shape
#       H2) — proving literal-kept, not implemented.
#     Shape H2 — `to_char(TIMESTAMP '2024-03-15
#       14:25:30', 'AM')` → 'AM' (real PG: 'PM' for
#       a 14:25:30 input). PROOF that AM is literal-
#       kept, not implemented: a 14:25:30 input is
#       afternoon, so the period marker should be
#       PM, not AM. zeta returns the literal `AM`
#       chars unchanged.
#     Shape I — `to_char(TIMESTAMP '2024-03-15
#       14:25:30', 'IYYY-IW-ID')` → 'I24Y-IW-ID'
#       (real PG: '2024-11-5' — ISO week-year, ISO
#       week, ISO day-of-week). Particularly bizarre
#       output: the parser matches the bare `Y` after
#       `I` as a year spec (returning `24`), then
#       leaves the surrounding `I` and `Y` literals
#       in place, then keeps `IW` and `ID` entirely
#       literal. The output `I24Y-IW-ID` mixes a
#       correctly-parsed year fragment with literal
#       spec chars.
#
#   NULL-passthrough support pin (well-behaved case):
#
#     Shape Z — `to_char(NULL, 'YYYY')` → SQL NULL.
#       The one shape in the function that matches PG
#       semantics. Pin it as the support case so any
#       future regression that ALSO breaks NULL
#       handling is caught.

--echo === Shape W1: to_char(1234.5678, '0000.0000') — works (digit-and-zero pattern) ===

# Baseline pin: the digit-AND-zero pattern works
# correctly (no overflow, no kept-literal chars). Lock
# in the WORKS subset so partial-impl drift is visible
# alongside any future regression of the working codes.

SELECT to_char(1234.5678, '0000.0000') AS works_zero_pattern;

--echo === Shape W2: to_char(DATE '2024-03-15', 'YYYY-MM-DD') — works (basic date format) ===

# Baseline pin: the YYYY/MM/DD spec works on a DATE
# input. This is the one shape pinned anywhere prior
# (function_basic.test:118). Re-pinning here makes the
# WORKS/KEPT-LITERAL contrast obvious within this
# slice.

SELECT to_char(DATE '2024-03-15', 'YYYY-MM-DD') AS works_basic_date;

--echo === Shape A: to_char(1, 'RN') — Roman numeral kept literally, returns 'RN' instead of 'I' (CORRUPTION) ===

# Real PG: '          I' (right-padded Roman numeral
# for the integer 1). zeta returns the literal `RN`
# chars unchanged — particularly insidious because
# the result looks like the user mis-pasted the
# format string into the value slot.

SELECT to_char(1, 'RN') AS rn_kept_literal;

--echo === Shape B: to_char(1234.5678, 'B99999') — B modifier kept literally, returns 'B 1234' instead of ' 1234' ===

# Real PG: ' 1234' (the B modifier means "render
# blanks if value is zero"; for a nonzero value it
# is non-printing). zeta keeps the `B` character in
# the output verbatim.

SELECT to_char(1234.5678, 'B99999') AS b_modifier_kept;

--echo === Shape C: to_char(1234.5678, 'FM999G999D99') — FM modifier kept literally, returns 'FM  1,234.56' ===

# Real PG: '1,234.56' (FM modifier strips padding,
# G is group separator, D is decimal point). zeta:
# the G/D codes happen to work (group sep / decimal
# pinned to ',' / '.' for the en_US locale), but
# the FM chars leak into the output AND the leading
# padding is NOT stripped (the FM functionality is
# absent, only its CHARACTERS are kept).

SELECT to_char(1234.5678, 'FM999G999D99') AS fm_modifier_kept;

--echo === Shape D: to_char(1234, '9999TH') — TH kept literally, accidentally matches PG output for digit 4 ===

# Real PG: '1234TH' (English ordinal for a number
# ending in 4 IS 'TH'). LOOKS correct but is
# coincidental — see Shape D2 for the proof.

SELECT to_char(1234, '9999TH') AS th_accidental_match;

--echo === Shape D2: to_char(1, '9TH') — TH kept literally, returns '1TH' instead of '1ST' (PROOF) ===

# Real PG: '1ST' (English ordinal for 1 is `ST`,
# not `TH`). zeta returns `1TH` — PROVES that TH
# is literal-kept, not implemented as the ordinal
# suffix.

SELECT to_char(1, '9TH') AS th_literal_proof;

--echo === Shape E: to_char(123, 'XXX') — hex format kept literally, returns 'XXX' instead of ' 7b' ===

# Real PG: ' 7b' (hex 7B for decimal 123, lowercased
# by default). zeta returns the literal `XXX` chars
# unchanged — no hex conversion happens.

SELECT to_char(123, 'XXX') AS xxx_hex_kept_literal;

--echo === Shape F: to_char(1234.5678, '999.99') — width overflow returns '###.##' instead of '1234.57' (CORRUPTION) ===

# Real PG: '1234.57' (PG widens the field rather
# than emitting `#` overflow chars; the `.99` slot
# count is a HINT to the rounding behavior, not a
# hard cap on the integer side). zeta emits three
# `#` chars on the integer side — apps that ported
# `to_char(num, '999.99')` from PG get unreadable
# output.

SELECT to_char(1234.5678, '999.99') AS overflow_hash_chars;

--echo === Shape F2: to_char(1.5e10, '9.99EEEE') — EEEE not implemented + overflow combine, returns '#.##' ===

# Real PG: '  1.50e+10' (scientific notation via
# the EEEE spec). zeta: combined fault — EEEE is
# stripped (not literal-kept here, inconsistent
# with the kept-literal behavior of other codes),
# AND the 1-digit integer slot overflows, so the
# output is `#.##`. THREE inconsistencies stack:
# (1) EEEE silently ignored not literal-kept,
# (2) overflow chars `#` emitted, (3) the resulting
# string has no resemblance to either a scientific
# notation OR a literal.

SELECT to_char(1.5e10, '9.99EEEE') AS eeee_combined_fault;

--echo === Shape G: to_char(DATE '2024-03-15', 'Day, DD Month YYYY') — Day/Month kept literally (HEADLINE date corruption) ===

# Real PG: 'Friday   , 15 March      2024' (padded
# weekday and month names). zeta: 'Day, 15 Month
# 2024' — the `Day` and `Month` full-name specs
# are kept LITERALLY. DD and YYYY (implemented)
# work correctly. Report templates emit the
# literal strings `Day` and `Month` instead of
# weekday/month names — silent garbage in PDFs,
# CSVs, audit logs.

SELECT to_char(DATE '2024-03-15', 'Day, DD Month YYYY') AS day_month_kept;

--echo === Shape H: to_char(TIMESTAMP, 'HH12:MI:SS AM') — HH12 digits run together + AM kept literally ===

# Real PG: '02:25:30 AM' (for a 02:25:30 input).
# zeta: '0212:25:30 AM' — two separate faults: the
# HH12 spec emits `02` but the `:` separator is
# consumed by the parser attempting `MI` (which
# also matches later), so the digits run together
# producing `0212`; AND the `AM` period marker is
# kept LITERALLY. See Shape H2 for proof of the
# AM literal-kept mechanism.

SELECT to_char(TIMESTAMP '2024-03-15 02:25:30', 'HH12:MI:SS AM') AS hh12_am_combined;

--echo === Shape H2: to_char(TIMESTAMP afternoon, 'AM') — AM kept literally, returns 'AM' for a PM time (PROOF) ===

# Real PG: 'PM' (14:25:30 is afternoon, so the
# period marker is PM). zeta returns the literal
# `AM` chars unchanged — PROVES the period marker
# is literal-kept, not implemented as the AM/PM
# resolver.

SELECT to_char(TIMESTAMP '2024-03-15 14:25:30', 'AM') AS am_literal_proof;

--echo === Shape I: to_char(TIMESTAMP, 'IYYY-IW-ID') — ISO week-year family kept literally with bizarre partial parse ===

# Real PG: '2024-11-5' (ISO week-year 2024, ISO
# week 11, ISO day-of-week 5). zeta: 'I24Y-IW-ID'
# — particularly bizarre output. The parser
# matches the bare `Y` after `I` as a year spec
# (returning `24`), then leaves the surrounding
# `I` and `Y` literals in place. The remaining
# `IW` and `ID` specs are kept entirely literal.
# Result: a string that mixes a correctly-parsed
# year fragment with literal spec chars.

SELECT to_char(TIMESTAMP '2024-03-15 14:25:30', 'IYYY-IW-ID') AS iyyy_partial_parse;

--echo === Shape Z: to_char(NULL, 'YYYY') — NULL passthrough (well-behaved, matches PG) ===

# The one shape in the function that matches PG
# semantics. Pin it as the support case so any
# future regression that ALSO breaks NULL handling
# is caught alongside the partial-impl drift.

SELECT to_char(NULL, 'YYYY') IS NULL AS null_input_returns_null;
